<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<opml version="2.0">
<head><title>Alpha Hours BrainLift</title></head>
<body>
    <outline text="BrainLift: Alpha Hours">
      <outline text="Owner">
        <outline text="Nancy Wisniewski Torvund">
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Purpose">
        <outline text="Primary purpose">
          <outline text="Design, launch and run Alpha Hours: a premium after-school program that delivers the Alpha day, mastery-based academics on Timeback followed by a life skills workshop, inside existing Alpha campuses, and sells it to three kinds of families at once: families who will never enroll at Alpha and want the best after-school program in the city, families who are considering Alpha and want to try it before they commit, and families Alpha has told &quot;not yet&quot; who want a paid, measured path back to yes. Current Alpha students, whose school day ends at 3:30, enroll on the same terms.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Families attend four hours a week: a two-night track (Monday/Wednesday or Tuesday/Thursday, 4:00 to 6:00 pm) or a single four-hour Saturday block (two are offered). Every block runs Timeback in pomodoros, 25-minute mastery sessions, ends with life skills and shoutouts, and families enroll one seven-week session at a time, five sessions a year.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Alpha Hours launches in Session 2, October 19 to December 18, 2026, in four markets and six campuses: New York (180 Maiden Lane, 100 seats, and 156 William Street, 30 seats), Greenwich, Boston (two campuses), and Chicago. 444 seats, 1,776 enrolled at capacity, a $40M revenue run rate on day one, $78M from January 2027 when the Chicago expansion lands.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="The program exists to do four things at once">
          <outline text="Make money. A premium after-school offering priced above every competitor, sold to every family who will pay for it, whether or not they ever apply to Alpha. The program is a P&amp;L first and a funnel second, and it clears its P&amp;L at zero conversions.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Use real estate we already pay for, turning empty late-afternoon, evening and weekend hours in Alpha buildings into revenue.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Feed Alpha. Give families a paid way to try the Alpha model and convert the ones who want more into full-time enrollment. Give families we have said &quot;not yet&quot; to a paid roadmap back, on data instead of a second interview.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Serve Alpha&#x27;s own families. Almost every Alpha campus in the country ends the school day at 3:30 with nothing behind it. Alpha Hours closes that gap on every campus, and gets paid a premium price to do it. Current Alpha students enroll at the same session price with a 5 percent family discount, and transition directly on campus at the end of the school day.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Success is measured on all four: site profit and revenue, building utilization, conversions to full Alpha, and current-family enrollment. The Head of Alpha Hours is reviewed on the P&amp;L and on full-time enrollments, both, every session.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Three lanes, one room">
          <outline text="Every family sits in one of three lanes. The room, the guides, the block and the price are the same in every lane. What differs is what we sell the family, what we measure, and what the family is worth.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Stay. Families who never plan to enroll at Alpha. Their child is at a school they like, or a school they are stuck with, and they want the best two hours in the city after it. This is the biggest lane, the one with no ceiling on tenure, and the one the old plan treated as a risk. A Stay family is a $22,500-a-year customer for as long as the child is in K-8, and we sell them exactly that: the Alpha day, after school, every session, for years.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Considering. Families who are deciding on Alpha. They pay $4,500 and four hours a week for seven weeks to try it, they get a mastery map and a weeks-to-grade-level projection at the end, and the structured conversion conversation fires on data. Some convert. The ones who do not are not lost. They move into Stay.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Path to yes. Families Alpha has told &quot;not yet.&quot; Today that is a letter and a roadmap and a lost family. Now it is a seat, a roadmap with measurable thresholds, a re-evaluation date, and seven weeks of data at a time, and the family pays for it. When the data clears the bar, the child is invited to apply. If it never does, the family is still a Stay customer.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="A fourth group, current Alpha families, enrolls on the same terms at a 5 percent discount and is the base load on every campus from day one.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Underneath all four sit Alpha&#x27;s three commitments, and every one carries into Alpha Hours with its own measurement.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Kids love school. Underneath everything sits Alpha&#x27;s first commitment, kids love school, and it carries into Alpha Hours unchanged: children love coming. This is measured by session-to-session re-enrollment and attendance, in addition to student surveys, and it is the leading indicator for everything else. A child who begs to come back is what fills the cohorts, climbs the mastery data, keeps the Stay family for years, and converts the Considering family. Profit and conversion are the outcomes, love of the program is the engine, and we track it accordingly.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Learn 2x. Students learn at the accelerated pace Alpha holds itself to during their academic minutes. Measured by following every student&#x27;s academic progress and learning velocity in Timeback, session over session. We do not promise 2x learning in one session, but promise the roadmap to 2x learning should the student enroll full-time at an Alpha School.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Learn life skills. Every session culminates in an AlphaTest Showcase, where students are tested on the life skills they have been working on, in front of their parents. The students will already be convinced by the time they have the showcase, it exists as a mechanism for convincing the parents.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Out of scope">
          <outline text="Custom changes to Timeback for after-school use. The program runs the platform as built, not subject-by-subject.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Certified-teacher tutoring models or one-on-one private tutoring. Alpha Hours cannot accommodate students who need 1:1 support, and says so publicly on the landing page.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Test-prep-only offerings (SAT and ACT, APs).">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Non-academic enrichment (arts, sports, social clubs) as the core product.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Full-day or full-time schooling. This is strictly after-school, Saturday, and school-break weeks.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Online-only delivery. The program is in-person inside Alpha buildings.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Childcare or custodial care. Alpha Hours is academic instruction, structured to qualify as instruction under state exemptions.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Subjects beyond Timeback. Grades outside K-8 at launch; the middle and high school evening block launches Session 4 (February 2027) in New York and Greenwich and everywhere else in Fall 2027.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Experts">
        <outline text="MacKenzie Price, co-founder of Alpha School">
          <outline text="Co-founded Alpha in Austin in 2014 and is the public voice of the model. Built a private-school network that puts core academics on an AI platform for about two hours a day, growing from about 200 students to over 1,000 in two years across a fast-expanding campus network.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Why follow: She proved the Alpha model draws families at $40,000 to $75,000 tuition and that parents will pay for measurable results delivered by software plus guides, not teachers. Alpha Hours is her model, moved into the after-school hours.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Toru Kumon, founder of Kumon">
          <outline text="Built one of the largest after-school brands in history, 25,000+ centers, on a simple idea, to master before you progress, and make progress visible to parents.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Why follow: He showed a tightly standardized, low-touch mastery system scales globally without certified teachers, and that most of his customers never wanted anything more than Kumon. Kumon is the volume baseline Alpha Hours must beat on outcomes and price, and the proof that the Stay lane is a business on its own.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Larry Martinek, co-founder of Mathnasium">
          <outline text="Turned classroom math methods into a franchise built on trained non-teacher instructors and a fixed method.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Why follow: His model proves the market pays a premium for structured help delivered by trained guides rather than credentialed teachers, which is exactly Alpha Hours&#x27; staffing thesis.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Raymond and Eileen Huntington, founders of Huntington Learning Center">
          <outline text="Built one of the first broad-subject tutoring franchises, anchored on diagnostic assessment and structured programs.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Why follow: They showed assessment data builds trust and justifies higher prices. Alpha Hours replaces their diagnostics with continuous Timeback mastery data, which is stronger proof delivered more often.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Michelle Rose Gilman, founder of Fusion Academy">
          <outline text="Grew a garage tutoring program into a one-to-one private school charging roughly $8,000 to $17,000 per course per year.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Why follow: Fusion is the current price ceiling for structured academic programs below full private school, and the reference price class Alpha Hours should be compared against, not Kumon.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Command Education and Lindamood-Bell (premium-pricing reference)">
          <outline text="Command Education charges $120,000 a year, up to $750,000 for a six-year package. Lindamood-Bell charges over $10,000 for a six-week reading intensive.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Why follow: Both price against the value of the outcome, not hours delivered. They are the template for Alpha Hours&#x27; pricing logic: anchor on the result, quote the session number, not hourly number.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Spiky Points of View">
        <outline text="SPOV 1: The family that will never enroll at Alpha is worth $22,500 a year, and there are more of them than applicants.">
          <outline text="Every version of an after-school feeder treats the family that stays and never applies as a leak. It is the opposite. In every city we operate in, the families who can pay $4,500 a session and will never leave their current school outnumber the families who will ever apply to Alpha by ten to one or more. They are not a risk to the funnel. They are the business. A Stay family re-enrolls five times a year for as long as the child is in K-8, and costs us nothing to acquire after the first session. We sell them Alpha Hours on its own merits, and we stop apologizing for the ones who never convert.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPOV 2: It&#x27;s bad business to give away all of our trials for free when we can make money on them.">
          <outline text="Traditional school admissions gives free trials to children because the schools need the families more than the families need the school. Alpha Schools do this today, too, with free shadow days, staff time and resources given away. Alpha Hours inverts this. Alpha Hours funnels a portion of prospective families into a paid experience, monetizing the trial that otherwise would have been free. There&#x27;s an opportunity to make money here that we shouldn&#x27;t throw away.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPOV 3: Every pathway to yes outside of Alpha is losing us money.">
          <outline text="We can monetize our &quot;pathway to yes.&quot; At Alpha a no is never a no forever. For every student who is not admitted, it is a &quot;not yet&quot; with a pathway to yes. Currently, the pathway to yes is feedback and roadmap. We can take that and turn it into a monetized pathway to yes inside of Alpha. Students who are a &quot;not now&quot; for Alpha Schools can join Alpha Hours with clear feedback and a roadway that they pay for inside of Alpha, and where we can more thoroughly evaluate the child and his or her progress.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPOV 4: A pathway to yes with no way to measure it or keep the families in our funnel is worthless.">
          <outline text="We can make our &quot;pathway to yes&quot; concrete and measurable, generating future revenue. Our current pathway to yes is feedback and a roadmap. We can take this and make it concrete and measurable, while gathering more detailed data over time on each student.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPOV 5: One shadow day is inferior to seven weeks of data.">
          <outline text="Some students are stand out &quot;yeses&quot; on a shadow day, but many are not. The data we gather in the course of a few hours is inferior to data gathered over seven weeks. We can boost the quality of our student population by using Alpha Hours to gather more detailed data on students who are not stand out &quot;yeses.&quot;">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPOV 6: Alpha Hours is a stronger qualifier than $100 and some unmeasured level of interest.">
          <outline text="Currently, families apply with a $100 application fee, and are handed a free Alpha experience. $100 does not indicate ability or willingness to pay $40,000-75,000 per year. Alpha Hours would be our most powerful qualifier for families. The families are willing to invest $4,500 and four hours a week for seven weeks. This tells us that a family has a strong level of interest, and is willing to pay.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPOV 7: 100 minutes a week and a roadmap is superior to six hours a day and a letter grade.">
          <outline text="What 100 weekly minutes of Timeback produces something no school or human teacher in America can give a parent, a precise mastery map of where the child actually is in every subject, the child&#x27;s measured learning velocity, and a computed answer to the only question that matters, which is exactly how many weeks at full Alpha until this child is at grade level, or the next one. The traditional school has the child six hours a day for years and produces a report card guessing subjectively at all of this. Seven weeks with us produces the map and the timeline. Every family who pays for Alpha Hours is running an experiment that indicts the school they already write checks to. We do not compete with Kumon. We compete with six hours of no information, and we win in seven weeks.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPOV 8: Every building sitting empty after 3:30 PM is a waste of assets that we should be sweating.">
          <outline text="We have buildings, owned or leased, across the country that are sitting empty for the majority of hours in the week. We should be sweating our assets to maximum capacity. Alpha Hours is just the start of a path to making money on every single available hour.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="SPOV 9: New York goes first, not last.">
          <outline text="The safe plan held New York out of the pilot to protect the brand and the admissions pipeline. That protects the wrong thing. New York is where the demand is already measured (13 percent of admissions conversations ask for after-school unprompted), where the price is already proven (174 paid Hamptons registrations at $4,500), where the guide training happens, and where the Head sits. Holding it back for a year forfeits $11.7M of run-rate revenue and the network&#x27;s best conversion data to protect against a risk the guide training program and the week-1 exit process already carry. New York launches in Session 2 at 130 seats across two buildings.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Insights">
        <outline text="Insight 1: Alpha Hours turns admissions from a cost center into a P&amp;L, and the P&amp;L does not need admissions to work.">
          <outline text="Once trials are paid and the pathway to yes is monetized, admissions stops being pure spend: shadow-day staff time becomes billable program hours and the marketing dollars behind every inquiry are recovered inside the funnel itself. But the P&amp;L does not rest on that. Half or more of every room is Stay families who were never in the funnel and never will be, and they carry the site profit on their own. Conversion is upside on a business that already works. Routing still has to be policy, not improvisation: admissions needs a formal disposition step for every family, yes, not yet with an Alpha Hours seat, or Alpha Hours as the product. The moment routing is left to individual judgment, the revenue leaks back to free shadow days.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Insight 2: The &quot;not yet&quot; path to yes letter becomes a product.">
          <outline text="Today a not yet costs goodwill and loses the family to another school&#x27;s funnel. A not yet that ships with an Alpha Hours seat, a concrete roadmap of mastery targets, and a scheduled re-evaluation date converts a rejection into revenue, retention, and a second look on better data. The roadmap must name measurable thresholds, so re-application becomes a data check instead of a second subjective judgment.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Insight 3: Seven weeks of data should produce the admissions decision, so set the bar before the session starts.">
          <outline text="Before each session opens, we define exactly what a child needs to show to earn an invitation to apply: how much they mastered, how consistently they showed up and completed their sessions. Then the decision makes itself. By the end of seven weeks, the data says yes or not yet, the same way the week-1 exit process works: the standard is set in advance and the record makes the call, not a gut feeling. This is what makes &quot;a more thorough evaluation&quot; a real claim instead of a longer version of the same judgment call. And it means we can raise the quality of the students we admit without admitting fewer of them.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Insight 4: Each lane gets its own yardstick, or the numbers lie.">
          <outline text="A family that pays $4,500 and commits four hours a week for seven weeks has told us far more than a family that pays $100 and fills out a form. But if every family in the room is measured on conversion, the Stay lane looks like failure and the program looks like a leaky funnel, when it is the opposite. So each lane is measured on what it is for. Stay: re-enrollment, tenure in sessions, revenue per seat. Considering: conversion to full Alpha by number of sessions completed, and cost per enrollment, which turns negative because the family pays us while they decide. Path to yes: the share of families who clear their published thresholds and the share of those who are admitted. Current Alpha families: fill and re-enrollment. The Head reports all four every session, and is reviewed on the P&amp;L and on full-time enrollments together.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Insight 5: The data builds over time, and it moves with the child.">
          <outline text="A shadow day gives us a few hours of impressions, and they fade. Alpha Hours gives us weeks of real data on what the child has mastered and the life skills they learn, and the data grows every session. When that family enrolls at full Alpha, the data comes with them: the child starts on day one at exactly the right level, no testing, no ramp-up. The same data that tells us whether to admit the child also tells us where to start them. No other school&#x27;s trial produces either.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Insight 6: Sweating the assets has an order of operations. Alpha Hours is the first step in filling every empty hour, and each step after it gets cheaper.">
          <outline text="Alpha campuses sit empty most hours of the week. Alpha Hours fills the first slice, weekday afternoons and Saturdays, and in doing so puts a trained team, insurance, and an operating playbook in place at every campus, all carried by that first program. Once that foundation exists, every other empty block, the 6:15 to 8:15 pm middle and high school block, school-break weeks, early mornings, adult evenings, can be added without rebuilding it, so each new program starts at a higher margin than the one before it. The first two additions, the evening block and break weeks, land inside Year 1. Alpha Hours is not the whole building-utilization strategy. It is just the first block of it.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Insight 7: The parking risk is a pricing question, not a policy question.">
          <outline text="The old worry was that a family would treat Alpha Hours as Alpha at a third of the price and never enroll. Two things dissolve it. First, the family that parks is a Stay customer, and a Stay customer is worth $22,500 a year for years: parking is the product. Second, the family that would have enrolled at full Alpha and is parking instead is a Considering family whose conversion conversation fires on data, not on the Head&#x27;s judgment, so the invitation comes whether or not the Head wants to keep the seat. What is left is a price: from the sixth session, every seat reprices to $5,000 in premium markets ($4,000 standard) unless the family has an Alpha application on file, in which case the year-one rate holds. The application is the discount, not the requirement. A long-stayer is either pipeline or premium revenue, never a parked seat, and no family is ever exited for staying.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Insight 8: Fill the room first, then raise the price. The waitlist sets the price, not the Head.">
          <outline text="At $4,500 the pilot campuses sell out or they do not, and either answer is information. A cohort that closes a session with a waitlist is underpriced, and reprices by $500 for the following session, mechanically. A cohort that does not sell 10 seats two weeks out does not open, and its families roll into another cohort. Between those two rules the price finds the market at every campus without a single pricing meeting, and the pilot&#x27;s job is to find out how high it goes.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Knowledge Tree">
        <outline text="Category 1: How non-school learning environments drive academic gains">
          <outline text="Summary: Mastery pedagogy, standardized pacing, and visible progress let after school programs outperform classrooms, using process-driven personalization rather than teacher variation.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Kumon: Founded 1954. Worksheet-based mastery, non-certified staff, 25,000+ centers, $150 to $220 per subject per month. Personalization lives in the process (worksheets and standards), not the content.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Mathnasium: Founded 2002. Trained non-teacher instructors, fixed method, math only, $250 to $500 per month.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Fusion Academy: One-to-one private school, $8,000 to $17,000 per course per year. Proves premium willingness to pay for personalized academics.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Alpha / Timeback / Alpha Hours: Leverages AI to deliver personalized academics equivalent/superior to 1:1 tutoring with a human, keeping each child in his or her zone of proximal development in each subject, delivering 2x learning.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Insights: Mastery scales when the system, not the teacher, carries instruction. Parents validate trust through visible, measurable progress. Premium after-school is about quality of result, not quantity of hours. Alpha Hours will provide personalized academic reports at the start and end of every session.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Category 2: After-school business models and the feeder">
          <outline text="Summary: After-school programs scale through standardization, real-estate leverage, and, for a school-owned program, conversion to full enrollment. The volume is in families who never change schools.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Market size: US families already spend about $40B a year on tutoring and after-school combined: tutoring is an $18.9B industry growing about 11 percent annually with K-5 the fastest segment (IBISWorld 2025); after-school is $21.0B, with 7M children enrolled and 22.6M more whose families want a spot and cannot get one (Afterschool Alliance 2025). Tutoring participation rose from 19 to 24 percent of students between 2024 and 2025, and high-income families already spend up to $9,000 per child per year on tutoring and enrichment. Almost none of that spend comes from families shopping for a new school. It comes from families keeping the school they have and buying what it does not provide. Alpha&#x27;s full 40-campus network at capacity is $268M, well under 1 percent of what families already spend today. The constraint is buildings, not demand.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Demand evidence, bottom-up. After-school is unprompted inbound demand, not only a hypothesis. In NYC admissions conversations, families ask whether Alpha offers after-school without being prompted. In the last 14 days, 3 of 23 NYC admissions conversations included an unprompted ask for after-school, 13 percent of all conversations, with no marketing and no product to point to. Most recently last week on Wednesday, a family said they would continue with Alpha by trying it that way if after school existed. The same pattern held in my prior private-school sales role, where the after-school question came up at least biweekly across a national territory. The demand also runs inside our own walls: Alpha parents nationwide ask what happens after the school day ends at 3:30, and today the answer is nothing. These are families telling us the entry product they want to buy before we have built it. Price acceptance is already proven at this exact number: Alpha Summer in the Hamptons has 174 paid registrations at $4,500 per week, with one price objection total. Alpha Hours asks the same families the same number for seven weeks instead of one. New York also holds the sales list: the 1,000-plus HubSpot families from the Hamptons outreach, the 174 paid Hamptons families, and every family in the NYC admissions pipeline who was not a yes.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Huntington: Founded 1977. Diagnostic-driven, multi-subject, $40 to $75 per hour. The assessments build trust and justify the price.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Premium pricing precedent: Command Education $120,000 a year, up to $750,000 for a multi-year package; Lindamood-Bell $10,000+ for six weeks; Fusion $60,000+ full-time. All price against outcome value, not hours, and all quote the package, not the hour.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Feeder economics: A private school typically spends $3,700 to $5,800 in marketing to win one enrolled student (Cube Creative Design, 2026 Private School Marketing Budget Planning Guide: industry median $3,677, secondary $5,844). Alpha Hours flips that: the prospective family pays us $4,500 a session while they decide, so evaluating Alpha earns money instead of costing it. The trial is also far stronger than a shadow day. Every session ends with a conversion artifact. The child&#x27;s mastery map and a personalized projection of weeks-to-grade-level at full Alpha, so the parent leaves holding a number, not an impression. Private schools typically convert 5 to 8 percent of inquiries to enrollments (Spiral Marketing, day-school benchmarks), and an inquiry is a stranger with a phone number, while every Alpha Hours family has paid $4,500 and watched their own child&#x27;s mastery data climb for seven weeks. We model conversion at 8 to 12 percent of the Considering and Path to yes lanes, and Session 2 exists to replace that assumption with our own measured number. Each conversion is worth $160,000 to $325,000 in lifetime tuition, which is why the feeder pipeline ($230M to $340M nationally) is worth more than the program&#x27;s own profit.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Insights: Standardization plus assessment builds scalable trust. Real estate already controlled is the margin. For a school-owned program, the feeder is the largest source of value, and it only functions with an intentional, structured handoff. The Stay lane is the volume, the feeder is the upside, and the same room serves both.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Business Model and Operations">
        <outline text="The program">
          <outline text="An Alpha day runs primarily on two things: mastery-based academics on Timeback and life skills workshops. Alpha Hours compresses that whole day into one or two blocks. A weekday block is two hours, and requires a Monday/Wednesday or Tuesday/Thursday registration. Each weekday block is organized as this: a ten-minute Limitless launch, two 25-minute academic sessions on Timeback with a short break between, a 40-minute life skills workshop, and ten minutes of shoutouts. Each of the two Saturday blocks runs the same day at double length: four 25-minute academic sessions, an 80-minute workshop, and a snack break in the middle. Either way the child gets the same week: 100 minutes of focused, adaptive mastery academics and 80 minutes of life skills. Timeback delivers the instruction and adapts to each child. The guide holds focus, effort, and motivation, and leads the workshop. That is the whole model: the Alpha day, academics and life skills, in one or two blocks.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="The lanes in operation">
          <outline text="Every family answers one question at enrollment: are you considering Alpha full-time? Three answers: no, we love our school (Stay); yes, we are deciding (Considering); we applied and were told not yet (Path to yes, set by admissions, not the family). Current Alpha families are flagged from the roster. The lane is on the roster, the Lead Guide sees it, and the data can move a family between lanes: a Stay family whose child&#x27;s mastery map shows a two-grade jump gets the Considering conversation whether they asked for it or not, and a Considering family who declines twice moves to Stay and stops getting the pitch. What the lane changes is the conversation at the AlphaTest Showcase and the report the family receives. What it never changes is the room.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="The Stay family gets a re-enrollment link, the next session&#x27;s schedule, and a mastery report that shows the child&#x27;s progress against grade level, on Timeback data. The Considering family gets the same report plus the weeks-to-grade-level projection at full Alpha and the structured conversion conversation, which fires only when the published thresholds are met. The Path to yes family gets the report scored against their roadmap thresholds, and an invitation to apply the session they clear them.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Motivation and incentives">
          <outline text="Alpha Hours runs the same rhythm as Alpha, starting with academics in Timeback, followed by a life skills workshop. What is different from Alpha is the child&#x27;s day. The child has already been through six hours of regular school, and walks in tired at 4 pm, and needs a reason to give full effort. Alpha already has this reason, built into the model twice. The first is Timeback&#x27;s own promise, carried into every block: get through your academics with focus, and the rest of the block is an amazing life skills workshop, the part of the block built around things kids actually love. The academics are the shortest path to the good part, exactly the trade Alpha kids make every day. The second is the extrinsic motivational model, and we will mirror this in Alpha Hours. Students will earn the Alpha currency for XP earned in Timeback, and will have the opportunity to purchase items in the Alpha Hours Emporium. Both motivators become even more important for a child who has been through six hours of school: they keep the child working diligently, and boost love of Alpha Hours.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="AlphaTest Showcase">
          <outline text="Every session ends with an AlphaTest Showcase in the final week. Students are tested on the life skills they have worked on across the session&#x27;s workshops, and parents come to see it. The showcase does double duty: it is how the life skills commitment gets measured instead of assumed, and it puts every parent in the building in the same week they receive their child&#x27;s mastery map and weeks-to-grade-level report, which makes it the natural setting for the re-enrollment and full-Alpha conversation. No other after-school program ends a term by putting the child&#x27;s growth on a stage in front of the person who paid for it.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Schedule and tracks">
          <outline text="Runs Monday through Thursday afternoons and Saturday, inside existing Alpha campuses. The weekday block starts right where the Alpha school day ends, so the building never goes dark and every child is home for dinner.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Weekday schedule. Requires Monday/Wednesday or Tuesday/Thursday registration.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Guide setup: 3:30 to 3:45 pm (guides prepare, devices ready). Drop-off: 3:45 to 4:00 pm. Current Alpha students transition directly on campus at the end of the school day. Block: 4:00 to 6:00 pm. Dismissal and clean up: 6:00 to 6:15 pm.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Weekday block rundown (inside the two hours): Limitless launch: 10 minutes. Academic session 1 (25 minutes). Break: 5 minutes. Academic session 2 (25 minutes). Transition: 5 minutes. Life skills workshop: 40 minutes. Shoutouts: 10 minutes. Total 120 minutes.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Saturday schedule: Setup: 8:45 to 9:00 am. AM block: 9:00 am to 1:00 pm. Transition: 1:00 to 2:00 pm (staggered pickup and drop-off; no lunch served). PM block: 2:00 to 6:00 pm. Clean up: 6:00 to 6:15 pm.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Saturday block rundown (inside the four hours): Limitless launch: 10 minutes. Academic session 1 (25 minutes). Break: 5 minutes. Academic session 2 (25 minutes). Break: 15 minutes. Academic session 3 (25 minutes). Break: 5 minutes. Academic session 4 (25 minutes). Snack break: 15 minutes. Life skills workshop: 80 minutes. Shoutouts: 10 minutes. Total 240 minutes. Four academic sessions. Snacks only, no meals.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Every campus runs four cohorts a week: Monday/Wednesday, Tuesday/Thursday, Saturday morning, and Saturday afternoon. The same room and the same guide team serve all four, and every cohort gets the same four weekly hours.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Middle and high school block (Session 4, February 24, 2027, at New York Maiden Lane and Greenwich; all pilot campuses Fall 2027; national with the build): Weekdays 6:15 to 8:15 pm, Monday/Wednesday or Tuesday/Thursday, drop-off 6:00 to 6:15, dismissal 8:15 to 8:30. Same spine as the K-8 block: Limitless launch, two 25-minute academic sessions on Timeback, then Alpha Max and Alpha X programming fills the workshop slot, shoutouts to close. The block adds two cohorts per campus and lifts the enrolled ceiling from four times room capacity to six. Staffing runs as a second part-time guide rotation, sized per campus when the block launches.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Break weeks: the same room runs a full-day week, 9:00 am to 3:00 pm, in the school-break weeks between sessions, at the Alpha Summer price of $4,500 a week. The first is April 19 to 23, 2027, across every pilot campus. From Year 2, two break weeks a year at every campus. One seat per child, no cohort multiplier.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Capacity">
          <outline text="Room capacity is the seats filled at one time, set per campus. Enrolled students per campus is four times the room capacity: two weekday tracks plus two Saturday blocks. A 100-seat room fills to 400 enrolled students. The middle and high school block adds two more weekday cohorts, lifting the ceiling to six times room capacity. The operating goal is to fill every cohort to capacity, then reprice.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="New York runs two rooms. 180 Maiden Lane, the main campus, holds 100 seats, 400 enrolled. 156 William Street, the Early Learning Center, holds 30 seats, 120 enrolled, and is the early-years room: the youngest Alpha Hours students, kindergarten through second grade, in the building already set up for them, with the older students at Maiden Lane. Both buildings are in the Financial District, blocks apart, and share one Campus Coordinator.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Enrollment">
          <outline text="Five sessions a year, each about seven weeks (about 35 program weeks). Families sign up one session at a time, choosing a weekday track or a Saturday block, with a one-session minimum and a $500 non-refundable deposit at sign-up applied to the session fee. They re-enroll session to session, and re-enrollment for the next session opens in week 4 of the current one, before the general public. Families who want the year buy it: five sessions prepaid at $22,500 holds the seat and the track for the year, with first pick of cohort. No discount for prepaying, no sibling discount. Seats are scarce and priced accordingly.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Current Alpha students enroll on the same terms at the same session price with a 5 percent family discount. From the sixth session, every seat reprices to the continuation rate, $5,000 per session in premium markets and $4,000 standard, unless the family has a completed Alpha application on file, in which case the year-one rate holds. Nobody is required to apply. The application is the discount. No family is exited for staying. Where a cohort holds a waitlist, families with applications on file hold their seats and families without release them first. Current Alpha students are exempt from both. One session at a time lowers the barrier to trying Alpha and cycles far more distinct families through each seat than an annual plan, and the annual plan captures the families who already know they are staying.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Real estate">
          <outline text="The building is a sunk cost Alpha already carries, whether it owns the campus or leases it, with after-hours and weekend access either way. Late-afternoon and Saturday use adds only marginal cost. Marginal cost about $150 to $250 per weekday, about $400 per Saturday (custodial, HVAC and lighting, security). Because the host is an Alpha campus, the facility fee is largely intracompany.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Team">
          <outline text="Head of Alpha Hours: $400,000. Owns strategy, pricing, national rollout, campus relationships, guide hiring and training, the conversion targets, and the full P&amp;L. Reviewed every session on revenue, site profit, re-enrollment, and full-time Alpha enrollments.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Operations and Admin VP: $200,000. A remote role supporting every campus, and the Head of Alpha Hours&#x27; right hand: the Head owns strategy, growth, hiring, and the P&amp;L, the VP runs the machine underneath it.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="For each campus, the VP: Runs registration end to end: session sign-ups, lane tagging, cohort placement, waitlists, repricing, and the roster handoff to the Campus Coordinator before every session. Executes compliance: secures the written state exemption determination before the campus opens, keeps it current, and verifies every hire&#x27;s background checks are complete before day one. Places and maintains insurance per site: general liability, professional liability, workers&#x27; compensation, and sexual abuse and molestation coverage. Drafts and manages the host-campus facility-use agreement, and coordinates access, security, and custodial arrangements with campus leadership. Procures centrally: laptops, supplies, and Alpha Hours Emporium inventory, shipped to the Campus Coordinator. Reports weekly to the Head: fill rate by cohort and lane, attendance, re-enrollment, revenue, waitlist depth, and a consolidated per-site P&amp;L.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Across the network, the VP builds the launch checklist every new campus runs, manages the launch calendar with the Head, and prepares the reporting the Head takes to leadership. One remote VP covers the full pilot, and the role adds operations support underneath it as the national footprint grows.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Campus Coordinator per campus: part-time, a 50 percent role at $50,000. Inventory, responsible for laptop set-up and student accounts, coordination between admin and guides. A strong candidate pool is public school teachers looking for work: organized, experienced with children and school operations, and available in every metro. Coordinators run operations. New York&#x27;s two buildings share one.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Dedicated Alpha Hours guides: part-time, working only for Alpha Hours, at $50,000 a year. Weekdays run about 2.75 hours (3:30 to 6:15 with setup and dismissal), four days a week, plus a roughly 10-hour Saturday, about 21 staff-hours a week. Every guide completes the Alpha Hours training program before running a room: shadowing at Alpha NYC and an AI-first asynchronous training course. Guides are hired for their experience with children and trained out of teaching; the core discipline of the role is restraint. Guide hiring and training are owned by the Head of Alpha Hours.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Lead Guide per campus: $75,000. Manages the guides, in charge of onboarding and overall programming. Same number of working hours as guides, about 21 hours a week. Every building has one, William Street included.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Staffing: about 1 guide per 10 students, two-adult minimum, extra attention to the youngest. The same team serves all four cohorts.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Compliance">
          <outline text="Design and market as academic instruction, not childcare, to qualify for state exemptions. Avoid meals where they trigger licensing (Florida especially). Sign a formal host-campus facility-use agreement. Get a written exemption determination per state before opening: New York, Connecticut, Massachusetts, and Illinois before Session 2. Every block is curriculum-driven instruction end to end: timed mastery-academics sessions on Timeback and a structured life skills workshop. Flag the Saturday format and the break-week format specifically in each state&#x27;s written exemption request.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="FBI background checks on every hire: fingerprint criminal check, national sex offender registry, state child-abuse registry. Minimum two adults on site at all times.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Insurance: general liability, professional liability, workers&#x27; compensation, and sexual abuse and molestation coverage (excluded from most general liability policies, must be added).">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Pricing">
          <outline text="Per session, priced on value not hours. Standard markets: $3,500 per session, $17,500 per year. Premium markets: $4,500 per session, $22,500 per year. Current Alpha families: same session price with a 5 percent discount ($4,275 premium, $3,325 standard). Every pilot campus is priced at the premium tier ($4,500), Chicago included, matching the national capacity model. Continuation rate from the sixth session: $5,000 premium, $4,000 standard, waived to the year-one rate for families with an Alpha application on file. Break weeks: $4,500 a week. Waitlist repricing: any cohort that closes a session with a waitlist reprices by $500 for the following session.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="We sell a product and measurable academic outcomes, not hours. A session is four hours a week for seven weeks, about 28 hours. At $4,500 that is roughly $161 an hour, just under elite 1:1 tutors ($200 to $300), who cover one subject; we cover multiple, supervised, with the data to prove progress. A single session is four to five times the most expensive mainstream competitor for the same span. The full year is roughly a third (30 to 40 percent) of full Alpha tuition ($40,000 to $75,000). We quote the session and full-year numbers, not the hour.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="The price point is already market-tested: 174 paid Alpha Summer registrations at $4,500 per week, one price objection in 174 transactions. At $4,500 per seven-week session, Alpha Hours is the same number for seven times the duration.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Financials">
        <outline text="All figures at list price, premium tier ($4,500 per session, five sessions), K-8 blocks only unless stated. The 5 percent current-family discount trims totals by 1 to 2 percent depending on mix. Break weeks and the middle and high school block are shown separately as additions.">
        </outline>
        <outline text="Unit economics per campus">
          <outline text="Revenue = enrolled x $4,500 x 5 sessions. Enrolled = room capacity x 4.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Per-student cost per year: Timeback $150 x 5 sessions, supplies and rewards $30 x 5, payment processing 3 percent, local marketing $300. About $1,875 per student per year, $375 per student per session.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Guides: 1 per 10 concurrent seats, part-time at $50,000 (roughly 21 hours a week). The same guide serves all four cohorts, so one guide supports up to 40 enrolled students, about $1,250 of guide cost per enrolled student at even fill.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Per-campus fixed cost: Lead Guide $75,000, Campus Coordinator $50,000 (50 percent role), facility about $45,000 a year, insurance about $8,000. About $178,000 a year. William Street shares New York&#x27;s coordinator, so its fixed cost is about $128,000.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Contribution per enrolled student: $20,625 a year ($4,500 less $375, times five). Margin runs about 83 percent at capacity, higher at larger campuses where the fixed team spreads over more revenue.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
