        <outline text="Year 1 additions">
          <outline text="Break week, April 19 to 23, 2027: 867 seats across the pilot at $4,500 a week, $3.9M revenue at capacity, about $3.5M profit (a full-day guide week and supplies are the only costs). Two break weeks a year from Year 2, about $7.8M on the pilot footprint.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Middle and high school block, from Session 4 (February 24, 2027) at New York Maiden Lane and Greenwich: two more cohorts per campus, 200 enrolled at Maiden Lane and 160 at Greenwich, $8.1M a year of run rate, $3.2M inside Year 1 across Sessions 4 and 5 at capacity. Every pilot campus adds it in Fall 2027, which lifts the expanded pilot ceiling by 1,734 enrolled and $39.0M a year.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Year 1 pilot with additions, at capacity: about $62M revenue. At plan: about $52M.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Revenue by lane">
          <outline text="The room is the same in every lane, so revenue per seat is the same. What the lane changes is tenure, and what the family is worth beyond the session fee. Mix is a modeled assumption for the pilot: Stay 50 percent, Considering 30 percent, Path to yes 10 percent, current Alpha families 10 percent. Session 2 replaces it with the measured split.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Stay">
            <outline text="Share: 50%">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Families at expanded capacity: 1,734">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Session revenue a year: $39.0M">
            </outline>
            <outline text="What else the family is worth: Tenure. Three years at year-one then continuation rates is $72,500 a family, before break weeks.">
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="Considering">
            <outline text="Share: 30%">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Families at expanded capacity: 1,040">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Session revenue a year: $23.4M">
            </outline>
            <outline text="What else the family is worth: Conversion. Two sessions ($9,000) plus a 10 percent conversion at $240,000 lifetime tuition is $33,000 expected per family, and the non-converters move to Stay.">
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="Path to yes">
            <outline text="Share: 10%">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Families at expanded capacity: 347">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Session revenue a year: $7.8M">
            </outline>
            <outline text="What else the family is worth: Admission on data. Three sessions ($13,500) plus a modeled 20 percent admit rate at $240,000 is $61,500 expected per family.">
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="Current Alpha">
            <outline text="Share: 10%">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Families at expanded capacity: 347">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Session revenue a year: $7.4M (after 5 percent discount)">
            </outline>
            <outline text="What else the family is worth: Retention. The school day no longer ends at 3:30.">
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="Conversion rates are modeled, not measured. The program clears its P&amp;L at zero conversions, so every conversion is upside on top of the session revenue above.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Downside economics">
          <outline text="Contribution per enrolled student is about $20,625 a year. Minimum operating cost per campus is about $278K, so break-even is 14 enrolled students per campus, about 4 per cohort, under 4 percent of full capacity at the largest sites. Enrollment closes before each session starts and guides are hired to sold seats, so every staffing step is taken against booked revenue, and site margin holds between 70 and 83 percent at every fill level above break-even. Exposure is capped by design: one-session enrollment, part-time staff, no capex, and a building Alpha already pays for mean the maximum downside at any site is seven weeks of payroll.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Two mechanical floors sit under it. Cohort floor: a cohort that has not sold 10 seats two weeks before session start (October 5, 2026 for Session 2) does not open, and its families roll into the other weekday track or a Saturday block. Site floor: a campus below break-even at week 3 of Session 2 (the week of November 2) closes at session end, staff released, room returned to the campus. Week 3 is the checkpoint because attendance and re-enrollment intent, the program&#x27;s own leading indicators, are both visible by then.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="The national build">
          <outline text="The build runs in three waves behind the pilot, each one a session apart, at capacity figures.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Pilot">
            <outline text="Session: Session 2, Oct 2026">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Campuses added: NYC (2), Greenwich, Boston (2), Chicago">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Seats added: 444">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Enrolled added: 1,776">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Run rate added: $40.0M">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Network run rate: $40.0M">
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="Wave 2">
            <outline text="Session: Session 3, Jan 2027">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Campuses added: Chicago expansion, Austin (300), Miami (150), Palm Beach (60), Miami Beach (50)">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Seats added: 983">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Enrolled added: 3,932">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Run rate added: $88.5M">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Network run rate: $128.4M">
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="Wave 3">
            <outline text="Session: Session 4, Feb 2027">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Campuses added: Every remaining campus, about 30, at a conservative 50 seats each">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Seats added: 1,550">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Enrolled added: 6,200">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Run rate added: $139.5M">
            </outline>
            <outline text="Network run rate: $267.9M">
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="Full network at capacity, about 40 campuses, K-8 blocks only: 11,908 enrolled, $267.9M revenue, about $223M site profit (about 83 percent), about $219M net after central team. Chicago: 2,068 enrolled, $46.5M. Austin: 1,200 enrolled, $27M. New York: 520 enrolled, $11.7M. The middle and high school block lifts every participating campus ceiling by 50 percent from Fall 2027, a path toward $400M+ at full capacity, and two break weeks a year across 2,977 seats add about $27M.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Year 1 network ceiling with every wave on schedule and every seat sold: $140.9M revenue (Session 2 $8.0M, Session 3 $25.7M, Sessions 4 and 5 $53.6M each). That is a ceiling, not a plan. The plan is the pilot table above, and each wave is released by the week-3 checkpoint of the session before it.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Market size">
          <outline text="TAM about $40B a year: what US families already spend on tutoring and after-school combined ($18.9B tutoring, $21.0B after-school), before any category growth. SAM about $16B a year: the share of that spend in Alpha&#x27;s metros, roughly 40 percent of premium households. SOM today about $268M a year: the full 40-campus network at capacity on K-8 blocks, about 1.7 percent of SAM. The constraint is buildings, and Alpha adds about 20 a year.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="The feeder">
          <outline text="One conversion to full Alpha is worth $160,000 to $325,000 in lifetime tuition. Modeled conversion 8 to 12 percent of the Considering and Path to yes lanes, replaced by our own measured number after Session 2. The program is profitable even at zero conversions, so the effective acquisition cost per full enrollment is negative.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Feeder sensitivity. At a $240,000 midpoint lifetime tuition value, on the pilot&#x27;s 3,468 enrolled at expanded capacity: 4 percent conversion is 139 enrollments, about $33M; 8 percent is 277, about $67M; 12 percent is 416, about $100M. On the full national base of 11,908 enrolled: 4 percent is about $114M, 8 percent about $229M, 12 percent about $343M. Even at half the modeled rate the feeder outearns the program&#x27;s own profit, and at zero the program still clears its P&amp;L.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Potential Objections and Challenges">
        <outline text="Reputation, and why New York is in">
          <outline text="Alpha Hours will be many families&#x27; first Alpha experience, and it carries the school&#x27;s brand into every one of them. A weak session damages the school and the feeder, not just the program. The earlier plan answered that by holding New York out of the pilot for a year. This plan answers it with the controls, at every campus, New York first: the guide training program runs at Alpha NYC, so New York&#x27;s guides are the best-trained in the network; guides are staffed to sold seats so no room ever opens understaffed; the Lead Guide owns quality in every building; the week-1 exit process protects every room from the one child who would wreck it; and the AlphaTest Showcase puts the proof on a stage every seven weeks. New York is also where the Head sits, where the demand was measured, and where the sales list already exists. The market that can be watched most closely is the wrong one to hold back.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Wrong-fit students, and the week 1 exit process">
          <outline text="One student who cannot function in a 1:10 room destroys the product for the nine other families who paid $4,500, and at this price that is not survivable. So the exit trigger is behavioral and mechanical, not a judgment call. Two things trigger it: behavior, every incident that pulls a guide off the room, disrupting another student&#x27;s session, refusing to remain in the block, is logged the moment it happens, and two logged incidents in week 1 is an exit before week 2. And level of support, a child who requires a level of individual support that compromises the program&#x27;s ability to deliver the three commitments, kids love school, learn 2x, learn life skills, to the rest of the room. The Head of Alpha Hours delivers the decision to the family, the log makes it. The family receives the incident record and a prorated refund. Both standards are published at enrollment, so an exit is the program enforcing what every family in the room paid for.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Students who need 1:1 support">
          <outline text="The staffing model is 1 guide per 10 students, and it cannot deliver one-to-one support. This is publicly listed on the landing page before any family pays: Alpha Hours cannot accommodate students who need 1:1 support. Stating it up front protects the ratio, sets expectations honestly, and filters at the top of the funnel instead of in week 1.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="The arrival window for non-Alpha students">
          <outline text="Outside schools dismiss around 3:00, and drop-off closes at 4:00. For most target families this is routine, and for the rest the two Saturday blocks carry the load. The window is held at 15 minutes on purpose: the block starts on time for everyone, every time.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Saturday competes with youth sports">
          <outline text="Saturday morning is prime league time in exactly our demographic. Two Saturday blocks, two weekday tracks, and session-at-a-time enrollment let families rotate around sports seasons instead of choosing against them permanently.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="The parking risk">
          <outline text="Some families will treat Alpha Hours as Alpha at a third of the price and delay full enrollment. For most of them that is the product, and they are Stay customers worth $22,500 a year for years. For the family that would have enrolled full-time, the structured conversion conversation, the mastery map, and the weeks-to-grade-level projection make the ceiling explicit: 100 minutes a week produces the map, full Alpha produces the acceleration. And from the sixth session the seat reprices to $5,000 unless an application is on file, so a long-stayer is either pipeline by definition or premium revenue, and where a waitlist stands, non-applicants release the seat first.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Six campuses at once is a lot for a launch">
          <outline text="It is four markets inside one flight radius, a shared guide pool across New York, Greenwich, and Boston, one remote VP, and one launch checklist. The two New York buildings share a coordinator and are blocks apart. Every campus opens on sold seats, so a slow market opens small and a fast one opens full, and the cohort and site floors decide which is which without a meeting. What six campuses buy is six measured conversion and re-enrollment numbers in one session instead of four, and a New York number, which is the one the network&#x27;s largest pipeline actually needs.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Guide supply at national scale">
          <outline text="Guide hiring and training are owned by the Head of Alpha Hours. The candidate pool, public school teachers and retired teachers, exists at scale in every metro, and the Alpha Hours training program, shadowing at Alpha NYC plus AI-first asynchronous training, standardizes quality before any guide runs a room.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Rollout">
        <outline text="Phase 1 (Session 2, October 19 to December 18, 2026): four markets, six campuses, sold to all three lanes">
          <outline text="New York (180 Maiden Lane, 100 seats; 156 William Street, 30 seats), Greenwich (Armonk, 80), Boston (two campuses, 40 and 100), Chicago (94 until the expansion lands). Every campus opens at full capacity, all four cohorts on sale, no cap and no small-group phase: the pilot enrolls every family these markets will buy, in every lane. 444 seats, 1,776 enrolled at capacity, a $40M run rate.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Sales open in September against lists that already exist: the New York admissions pipeline (every family who was not a yes gets a Path to yes seat or a Considering seat with the not-yet letter), the 1,000-plus HubSpot families from the Hamptons outreach, the 174 paid Hamptons families, current Alpha families in every market, and the UHNW admissions event lists in Greenwich and Boston. Cohort floor decision October 5. Week-3 checkpoint the week of November 2. Re-enrollment for Session 3 opens in week 4, the week of November 9, to enrolled families first.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Launching at Session 2 rather than Session 1 gives campuses a settled start to the school year and the runway for guide hiring, state exemption determinations (New York, Connecticut, Massachusetts, Illinois), and insurance. Quality is carried by the guide training program at Alpha NYC, the Lead Guide at every building, and guides hired to sold seats so no room opens understaffed.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Pilot success criteria and measurement plan">
          <outline text="The pilot succeeds on three measured numbers: session-over-session re-enrollment at or above 70 percent, plan fill (60 percent at launch, 75 percent in Session 3), and conversion to full Alpha at or above 8 percent of the Considering and Path to yes lanes. The first two are the business. The third is the upside, and it is measured with the same rigor. The experiment is instrumented before it opens, in five commitments.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="The lanes and the funnel are defined before Session 2 starts. Every family is tagged to a lane at enrollment. Conversion is not one number; it is tracked per family through six stages: paid Alpha Hours enrollment, structured conversion conversation held, application submitted, admitted, full-Alpha enrollment signed, first tuition payment. Stage-level tracking is what says whether a weak result is a marketing problem, an admissions problem, or a product problem. Stay families are tracked on tenure, not conversion.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="The trigger is mechanical. The thresholds that fire the conversion conversation and the application invitation, mastery gains, and session completion rate, are published before Session 2 opens, so the measured rate reflects the product, not the persuasiveness of whichever Lead Guide ran the conversation that week.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="The number is lift, not a raw count. Conversion is measured against a matched baseline of non-Alpha-Hours inquiries from the same markets and the same window, because some share of these families would have applied anyway, and the case rests on what Alpha Hours adds, not on conversions that were coming regardless.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="Sample size is respected. Session 2&#x27;s 1,776 families are reported as directional, with the margin of error stated; the conversion read runs on the full 3,468 base by Session 3. Every number is reported by campus, by cohort, by lane, by session count, and by market, and a material spread between Chicago and the East Coast markets is treated as a market-mix finding that reshapes rollout sequencing, not noise to average away.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="The decision rule is pre-committed, and it runs on the business, not on conversion. Wave 2 is released at the Session 2 week-3 checkpoint if fill across the pilot is at or above 40 percent and no campus is below its site floor. Wave 3 is released at the Session 3 week-3 checkpoint on the same test plus Session 2 re-enrollment at or above 60 percent. Conversion below 6 percent at Session 3 does not pause the build; it triggers a funnel-stage review and moves the admissions conversation, not the program. Above 10 percent, the feeder becomes the lead story to leadership. Session 3&#x27;s leadership conversation is the numbers and what they trigger, not another debate.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Phase 2 (Session 3, January 4, 2027): the Chicago expansion and the second wave">
          <outline text="Chicago&#x27;s campus expansion lands and Alpha Hours steps with it, from 94 seats to 517, lifting the enrolled ceiling from 376 to 2,068 and the pilot from 1,776 to 3,468. One session of Chicago operating data, a waitlist built against the 94-seat footprint, and a trained guide bench sized to sold seats carry the step. This is the national build in miniature: the same room, the same playbook, five and a half times the seats, executed mid-pilot.">
          </outline>
          <outline text="The same session, Austin (300 seats), Miami (150), Palm Beach (60), and Miami Beach (50) open on the pilot playbook, released by the Session 2 week-3 checkpoint. Florida&#x27;s exemption request, which flags meals and the break-week format, is filed in September alongside the pilot states. Network at Session 3: 1,427 seats, 5,708 enrolled, $128.4M run rate at capacity.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Phase 3 (Session 4, February 24, 2027): national launch, and the evening block in New York and Greenwich">
          <outline text="Every remaining campus, about 30, opens at a conservative 50 seats with two full sessions of operating data and the first measured conversion numbers. The middle and high school block opens the same session at New York Maiden Lane and Greenwich, 6:15 to 8:15 pm, adding 360 enrolled and $8.1M of run rate. Network at Session 4: about 40 campuses, 11,908 enrolled on K-8 blocks, $267.9M run rate at capacity.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Phase 4 (April 2027 through Fall 2027): break weeks and the evening block everywhere">
          <outline text="The first break week runs April 19 to 23, 2027 across the pilot campuses, $3.9M at capacity. Fall 2027, the middle and high school block launches at every pilot campus and across the network, lifting every participating campus ceiling by 50 percent, and two break weeks a year become standard at every campus. The network grows with every new campus Alpha opens, about 20 a year.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Why these campuses">
          <outline text="New York is the network&#x27;s most valuable pipeline and its best-instrumented market: measured unprompted demand, a proven $4,500 price, the guide training site, the Head on the ground, and two buildings blocks apart that let the youngest students run in the room built for them. Chicago is the network&#x27;s largest campus: 94 seats at launch, 517 from January 2027, 2,068 enrolled at capacity, so the pilot tests the model at the exact scale the national build depends on, and its mid-pilot expansion is a live rehearsal of scaling a site, in a market outside the East Coast corridor, which makes the measured numbers generalize. Greenwich and Boston are dense UHNW markets at premium tuition where every conversion is worth the most, close enough to New York that one launch team and a shared guide pool cover four buildings. Four markets, six campuses, one session: enough to measure every lane in every kind of market, and enough revenue that the answer pays for itself either way.">
          </outline>
        </outline>
      </outline>
    </outline>
</body>
</opml>
